  "slug": "untitled-topic-876d",
  "summary": "Simulation-trained surrogates now link physical models to optical design and blind PSF estimation in these preprints.",
  "synthesis": "The three preprints share a reliance on physics-based simulation to train or guide learned models for optical systems, although their roles differ. A surrogate-guided design of incoherent multispectral diffractive optical networks targets onboard satellite image classification, treating the optical hardware as a design variable rather than a reconstruction problem. A neural surrogate for scintillator light-guide photon transport, trained via adjoint Monte Carlo sampling, makes detector readout differentiable so that geometry and readout can be co-optimized. DeepBlur, trained on physical simulations, addresses the inverse problem instead, estimating spatially varying 2D point spread functions blindly from single microscopy images to improve deconvolution. What is new across the set is the use of differentiable or simulation-trained surrogates to couple forward physics with either design or inference, rather than a single shared modality. The dominant thread is therefore the surrogate-mediated coupling of physical models to computational optical imaging, not a common instrument.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
